version: "2.0"
name: gnomon-kernel-desk
description: >-
  The Desk: kernel folds and routers behind the GUI Desk. Desk guidance, offer
  routing between mods and sessions, Desk statistics, the per-turn economy view,
  and the MCP server passthrough and enable/disable cards.
context:
  - >-
    desk-guidance.ts, offer-router.ts, desk-stats.ts and turn-economy.ts are
    derived views over log rows; mcp-passthrough.ts, mcp-servers.ts and
    mcp-server-cards.ts handle user MCP servers.
  - >-
    The ^offer-accept and ^mcp-server-enable gates and the $offer-handoff flow
    stay in packages/gnomon/.purpose.
  - >-
    Split out of packages/gnomon/.purpose (2026-10-27); path: values stay
    relative to packages/gnomon, the package root.
components:
  desk-guidance:
    description: >-
      #desk-guidance (TD-2026-10-20-812 A1, slice S1; spec §12.9).
      src/kernel/desk/desk-guidance.ts: the frozen DESK_BODY (pinned
      DESK_BODY_HASH) and a kernel-composed roster from manifest fields only
      (mod name, offer kind, phase, title; A7), wrapped in <gnomon-desk>.
      composeGuidance(voice, desk) returns ONE block (voice first, blank line,
      desk) so table talk and the Desk share the single extra text block under
      one turn.sent{guidanceHash} that trace-cli extrasAgree admits (Aegis M2);
      voice alone is exactly TABLE_TALK_GUIDANCE. The supervisor's
      setTurnGuidance takes the composite (setVoiceGuidance is its alias).
    path: src/kernel/desk/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, desk, mod-playbooks, paradigm-8]
    flows: [$offer-handoff]
    tests:
      - test/desk-guidance.test.ts
      - test/table-talk-session.test.ts
    oracles: [G-DSK-01]
  offer-router:
    description: >-
      #offer-router (TD-2026-10-20-812, slice S1; spec §12.9).
      src/kernel/desk/offer-router.ts: a fold over committed rows (daemon
      log.onCommit, like the speakable projector; seed() replays offer.* and
      request.offer.mute at boot). When the main-chain reply ends
      (message_delta or an assistant line with stop_reason end_turn,
      parent_tool_use_id null; T-2026-10-21-006, since Claude Code can emit
      turn.result ~30 s later, after the next turn was sent) it parses the
      text of the turn's FINAL main-chain assistant message with
      offerBlockOf; a tool_use stop never evaluates, a sub-agent message is
      ignored, turn.result evaluates only a turn whose end was not seen, and
      each turn is evaluated once. At most one per reply, none while another
      turn sent before the reply ended is outstanding (a turn sent after it
      does not suppress it), none while the session has a pending card; the mod must be installed, granted, unpaused (DeskMods), not muted
      for the hsid (offer.decided not-now / stop-here) nor its repo
      (request.offer.mute, repo:<16 hex> from the session's repoKey), and the
      kind one of its declared offers; phase, title and contextIn are copied
      from the manifest (Aegis D3). It returns the kernel offer.shown row (the
      daemon appends it). decide(): request.offer.decide from the human on a
      gui/device connection only (Aegis D1), offer live (session running and
      owned by the asker), latest for the hsid, undecided (exactly one
      decision; a concurrent replay is refused before the row commits); writes
      request.offer.decide + offer.decided and on open sends the kernel
      template "Open <title>: use the <handoffTool> tool." as the human
      (session.inject origin kernel). offer.kept once per offer when the
      declared handoffTool on mcp__gnomon-<mod>__ completes without error
      after open. mute(): request.offer.mute row as the kernel. Aegis S1
      fixes: open names the fully qualified tool mcp__<server>__<handoffTool>;
      only kernel-principal rows fold; the next turn.sent retires the shown
      offer; decided once the request row commits (a retry is a replay, no
      second request row), send failures reported (diag send.failed); a mod
      paused or muted after open never counts as kept (diag kept.refused);
      mutes survive session exit until the bounded state is evicted. Every
      evaluation that shows nothing returns a kernel offer.suppressed{hsid,
      turn, why, name?, offerKind?} row for the signed log (founder ruling,
      contracts 8.0:T-2026-10-21-006; closed, checked at append by
      #kernel-log), why in no-block | turn-streaming | pending-card |
      not-offerable | kind-undeclared | malformed; name/offerKind only when
      the block named them; names only, never message text; at most one per
      completed turn, no-block only while the session has an offerable mod.
      Review M2: turn.result{turn} closes every outstanding turn up to the one
      it names (FIFO when absent). Review R3: session.exited, session.open and
      session.drained drop the turn state (outstanding turns, message text,
      evaluation marks; mutes stay, L7), since a resumed session restarts
      its turn ids at /1. Review R4: the turn.result fallback writes no row
      for an errored or interrupted turn (isError, or a subtype other than
      success). Review R1: each outstanding turn is a record evaluated at most
      once; a reply's end is attributed to the oldest outstanding turn not
      yet evaluated (an interleaved stream no longer loses the next turn's
      offer); "others" counts only outstanding turns still streaming (not yet
      evaluated); turn.result keeps the text of a newer turn's message.
      Review R2: a message_delta end_turn is evaluated at its message_stop; a
      new main-chain message in an evaluated turn before its turn.result (a
      Stop-hook continuation) or a blocking Stop hook_response
      (stopHookBlocked, founder 2026-10-23: buildClaudeArgv passes
      --include-hook-events, so the lines arrive; measured on claude 2.1.309
      the block is exit_code 2 or exit 0 with stdout JSON decision block,
      never a top-level decision or outcome 'blocked'; cancelled = a timeout
      and exit 1 never block; Aegis M2: the kernel row's hook facts decide
      first, the line is parsed only for a row without them) retires the
      shown offer and re-opens the turn; replay lines (isReplay) mark turns
      started, so the stream's turn is exact and a turn folded into a later
      one settles without a row. Re-review L-1: with replay lines an
      unconsumed queued turn is not "streaming"; without them a message after
      every outstanding turn is evaluated continues the previous message's
      turn. Re-review L-2: replay lines before the stream is live (the
      first system.init or turn.sent since a session boundary) are ignored,
      in the router and the supervisor's consumedTurns. Re-review M-2: a retired offer that was
      still undecided is the kernel row offer.retired{offerId, hsid,
      why:'continued'} (OFFER_SEED_KINDS: folded at boot), and a decide on
      it is refused 'retired'. Review C2: gnomon desk stats folds only
      kernel-principal offer.shown / offer.suppressed rows. Review C3
      (verified): desk stats key on offerId (ULID), mod and hsid, the GUI
      slips on offerId anchored by seq, and the router and the GUI drop
      turn state at session.open, so turn ids restarting at /1 after a
      resume collide nowhere. Pause
      and mute stop offers at once; removing the server applies to new
      sessions. CLI
      `gnomon desk stats` (src/cli/desk-cli.ts) folds shown/suppressed (by
      reason)/open/not-now/stop-here/kept per mod from the verified log, and
      the unnamed suppressions (no-block, malformed) per session; re-review
      M-3: a retired, undecided offer leaves shown (counted as retired) and a
      suppression counts once per (hsid, session.open seq, turn).
    path: src/kernel/desk/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, desk, mod-playbooks, paradigm-8]
    flows: [$offer-handoff]
    gates: [^offer-accept]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/offer-router.test.ts
      - test/desk-stats.test.ts
    oracles: [G-DSK-02, G-DSK-03, G-DSK-04]
  desk-stats:
    description: >-
      #desk-stats. src/kernel/desk/desk-stats.ts: the pure foldDeskStats /
      foldDeskUnnamed `gnomon desk stats` prints (moved from cli/desk-cli.ts,
      which re-exports them) and DeskTally, the kernel's live tally (seeded
      from the log once, fed by onCommit, bounded) behind
      status.mods.installed[].desk.
    tags: [desk]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/mods-admin.test.ts
      - test/desk-stats.test.ts
  turn-economy:
    description: >-
      #turn-economy (orchestration design §12 Phase 0 item 1, the `turn.economy`
      fold; spec id #turn-economy). src/kernel/desk/turn-economy.ts: a derived
      READ-ONLY VIEW (no log kind, no contract change) over turn.event /
      turn.sent / turn.result / session.open. One numbers-and-ids row per turn
      (a result that folds a queued turn is one row, foldedTurns 2): modelCalls
      (distinct main-chain message.id), preEditExplore {read, grep, glob} until
      the first Edit/Write/NotebookEdit, ctxAtStart (null for the first turn of
      an epoch, a resume included), cacheReadShare from turn.result split, and
      subagentTokensByModel (parent_tool_use_id lines, by message.model). Keyed
      (hsid, session.open epoch seq, turn) as #desk-stats is, since turn ids
      restart at /1 after a resume. Lines stored by hash only are counted as
      truncated in the coverage and never parsed; rewritten (lineKept) lines
      are not read. foldTurnEconomy is the pure fold; EconomyTally (daemon.ts,
      next to DeskTally) seeds from the human projection on first read and is
      fed by onCommit. CLI: `gnomon economy stats [<home>] [--json]`
      (src/cli/economy-cli.ts, folds incrementally inside verifyLog onRow).
      Review fields: ctxMax, callsOver140k, ctxHistogram (bounded buckets),
      conductorModel, subagentUncachedByModel, edited, ctxAtStartSource
      (prev | firstCall), hashOnly / truncatedAssistant / excludeFromMedians;
      model names sanitised; assistant lines with no outstanding turn are
      dropped (coverage.unpairedLines).
    tags: [desk, economy, paradigm-8]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/turn-economy.test.ts
  mcp-passthrough:
    description: >-
      #mcp-passthrough (TD-2026-10-20-812 A2, slice S1; spec §12.9).
      src/kernel/desk/mcp-passthrough.ts: DeskMods / readDeskMods (installed,
      pinned snapshot verifies, manifest matches its grant, capabilities and
      egress granted, not paused; cached on the grant table) and mergeDeskMcp,
      wired as SupervisorOptions.deskMcp into the session mcp.json: gnomon-<mod>
      (gnomon-<mod>-<entry> for extra entries), never a repo server name nor an
      earlier mod's, repo-muted mods skipped. Per-user launch override (Aegis
      D2): kernel state only (<home>/desk/mcp-overrides.json, 0600), never a
      repo file; `gnomon mod config <mod> --command <abs> --args … --env N=v`
      writes a request under <home>/mods/<mod>/ (session sandboxes cannot write
      the home); McpConfigCards raises a root-class grant card showing
      command, args, env NAMES and the launch hash; only allow writes the
      store. Accepted only for what provides.mcp[].config declares
      (validateMcpUserConfig) and an absolute command; a store entry failing
      the checks falls back to the manifest launch. Fail closed (Aegis S2):
      a server whose RESOLVED command is not absolute (isAbsoluteCommand:
      POSIX /… or a Windows drive path; // and UNC refused) is never added
      (skipped needs-config, daemon diag daemon.desk.mcp.unconfigured with
      the gnomon mod config hint); both shipped first-party defaults fail it
      until configured. S0b regression fix: the session base is the repo's
      VERIFIED GENERATED distro mcp.json (.gnomon/generated/.mcp.json, named
      gm_<pub>_<distro> by renderGenerated) and is passed as opts.distro /
      repoServersOnly(repo, distro): kernel output, so it bypasses the
      reserved-prefix drop that still applies to repo-authored names; a mod
      deriving the same name wins it (PassthroughResult.shadowed, diag
      mcp.distro-shadowed) and a distro server is never in `added` (H1/H1b
      intact). Names come from ONE helper, serverNameOf: an entry
      named <mod> or gnomon-<mod> is gnomon-<mod>, else gnomon-<mod>-<entry>
      (gnomon-neurons-brain); #offer-router's kept check uses the same helper.
      Aegis S1 fixes: repo servers named with a reserved desk prefix
      (RESERVED_DESK_PREFIXES gnomon-, gm_, gx_) are dropped (diag
      mcp.reserved); the daemon records the servers added per hsid and the
      router offers and counts kept only through them; no desk server before
      the router is bound (mcp.unbound); per added server a diag mcp.added
      {server, launchHash, overridden}; launchHashOf is an HMAC under the
      kernel key <home>/desk/launch-hash.key; overrides refuse secret-marked
      env names (manifest provides.mcp[].config.secret), non-.exe commands on
      win32 and args the card would truncate (MCP_CARD_ARGS_MAX); the card
      says env values land in the session's mcp.json and labels the requester
      "a request file under the Gnomon home".
    path: src/kernel/desk/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, desk, mcp, mod-playbooks, paradigm-8]
    tests:
      - test/mcp-passthrough.test.ts
    oracles: [G-DSK-05]
  mcp-servers:
    description: >-
      #mcp-servers (T-2026-10-23-003): the human's own MCP servers and the
      first-party Paradigm MCP in Gnomon sessions, still under
      --strict-mcp-config. src/kernel/desk/mcp-servers.ts discovers the
      servers Claude Code would load (~/.claude.json user + projects map,
      the repo .mcp.json, enabled user-scope plugins' plugin.json/.mcp.json
      with the root realpath'd inside ~/.claude/plugins), classifies each
      (problems: name, law-7, env-name, remote, command, plugin-path, args,
      transport, first-party; flags: shell wrapper, fetch-at-launch), shows
      rows with NAMES only (env, header, ${NAME} refs; secret-looking args
      hidden; URL without query), hashes each declaration with an HMAC under
      the kernel launch key, resolves the first-party @a-company/paradigm
      (paradigm-mcp bin inside the package) and composes a session's set
      (first party, then enabled servers whose hash still matches; ${NAME}
      values from ~/.gnomon/loop-env; missing ⇒ skipped env-missing).
      src/kernel/desk/mcp-server-cards.ts McpServers: the store
      <home>/desk/mcp-servers.json (0600), request.mcp.enable raising ONE
      grant card (detail.action mcp.enable; root class, hand-only, never
      standing; prepare refuses a changed declaration), request.mcp.disable
      and the first-party on/off with no card, status.mcp (names only),
      the per-session compose and the first-party write-denies
      (SupervisorOptions.mcpDenyWrite). A repo .mcp.json or local server is
      turned on for its own repo only. Windows: rows yes, enabling refused
      (root key). Every server runs inside the session sandbox (no nested
      SBPL, measured); the card names the host and says it has the
      session's network.
      TD-2026-10-23-361 (Aegis review, founder rulings): H1 the human's own
      ~/.claude/settings*.json allow rules for a server's mcp__ tools are
      honoured and NAMED (autoAllowRules) on the card, the row and the
      Paradigm row; Gnomon adds none. M3 the first party runs live from its
      resolved path, unpinned, linked shown (accepted residual: a session in
      a linked checkout can change the next session's Paradigm MCP, which
      runs under the same profile); every enabled `all` server's command and
      absolute args are write-denied in sessions, inside-roots exempt and
      logged (mcp.deny.skipped). M1 ${NAME} only in URL path and query (a
      loopback port excepted), placeholders shown, the expanded URL
      re-checked at compose. M2 law-7 references refused. L1 repo .mcp.json
      symlink / hard link / outside-repo refused. L2 secret NAME=value and
      short-flag values hidden. L3 inline code flagged. L5 mcp.user.added
      after the merge (noteMerged).
      #batch-card (founder 2026-10-23: "grouped cards of related things,
      one password"): request.mcp.enable{servers:[{id, scope, repo?}]}
      turns 1..MCP_ENABLE_MAX (16) servers on with ONE grant card
      (detail.servers[], rootRows []) and one fresh presence; each server is
      its own fenced field block (mcpBatchCardLines: what runs or where it
      connects, env and header names, takes-from, auto-allow rules, launch
      hash). Everything is validated first (servers[i] named in the
      refusal; nothing raised); over the count cap or MAX_CARD_CHARS
      (measured after wrapping, maxCardChars for tests) refused, never cut.
      A server already on is left out (unchanged); a batch overlapping a
      waiting card is refused (card-pending), the same batch again returns
      its card. At allow each launch hash is checked again: a changed server
      is skipped and reported (status.mcp.skipped, mcp.user.skipped-changed);
      when every server changed, prepare refuses before the presence prompt.
      A single {id, scope} is a batch of one with its one-server card.
      Turning off stays immediate with no card. status.mcp.max names the cap.
      Aegis Lows (2026-10-23): L1 skips kept per cardId (status.mcp.skips,
      newest last, at most MCP_SKIPS_KEPT 16, each with on[] and the deciding
      connId; status.mcp.skipped stays the newest), the event carries connId;
      L2 permission.ts fieldLines splits on every line break (\r\n, \n, \r,
      \v, \f, U+0085, U+2028, U+2029) so no line escapes a fence; L3 every
      enable card, one server too, carries the wrapped block (never cut).
    path: src/kernel/desk/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, desk, mcp, security, paradigm-8]
    signals: ['!mcp-server-enabled', '!mcp-server-disabled']
    gates: ['^mcp-server-enable']
    tests:
      - test/mcp-servers.test.ts
      - test/mcp-servers-sandbox.test.ts
    oracles: [G-MCP-01, G-MCP-02, G-MCP-03, G-MCP-04, G-MCP-05, G-MCP-06, G-MCP-07]
